After ~5 minutes of mining, back-to-back push notifications during a recovery window cause double-spawning (16 workers instead of 8), spiraling into a doom loop with recovery firing every ~2.4s.

## Changes

- **Remove `create_workers()` + `sleep_for()` from `recovery_initiated_handler`**  
  `set_block_handler` already has a just-in-time worker spawn guard in degraded mode. Pre-spawning in the recovery handler races with it: first recovery spawns 8, second push fires before the template arrives → `stop` → `create` (8 again) → first recovery's template lands → JIT guard sees 0 alive workers → spawns 8 more = 16 total.

  ```cpp
  // Before — premature spawn races with set_block_handler JIT guard
  solo_protocol->set_recovery_initiated_handler([this]() {
      mark_recovery_initiated("push_staleness");
      stop_all_workers();
      create_workers();   // ← causes double-spawn on back-to-back pushes
      std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(WORKER_INIT_DELAY_MS));
      retry_template_request(true);
  });

  // After — workers created only when recovery template actually arrives
  solo_protocol->set_recovery_initiated_handler([this]() {
      mark_recovery_initiated("push_staleness");
      stop_all_workers();
      retry_template_request(true);
  });
  ```

- **Fix `create_workers()` self-guard: skip instead of clear-and-respawn**  
  The existing guard on `!m_workers.empty()` was resetting and re-creating workers rather than bailing out early. Changed to `return` with a warning log, so any accidental concurrent call from two recovery paths is a no-op rather than silently replacing live workers.

