Every service property in `GitFlowCommand<TSettings>` used an expression body, silently constructing a fresh object graph on each access — meaning a single command execution could spin up several independent `GitService` and branch-service instances with no shared state.

## Changes

- **`GitFlowCommand<TSettings>`** — replaced expression-body service properties with null-coalescing lazy-init backing fields (`??=`) for `GitService`, `FeatureService`, `BugfixService`, `ReleaseService`, `HotfixService`, `SupportService`, and `Initializer`

```csharp
// Before – new instances on every access
protected IGitService GitService => new GitService(GitExecutor);
protected FeatureBranchService FeatureService => new(GitService);

// After – created once per command invocation, shared across all accesses
private IGitService? _gitService;
protected IGitService GitService => _gitService ??= new GitService(GitExecutor);

private FeatureBranchService? _featureService;
protected FeatureBranchService FeatureService => _featureService ??= new(GitService);
```

This ensures all code within a command invocation operates on the same object instances, and eliminates redundant allocations (e.g. `ExecuteList` previously called `service.List()` and `GitService.GetCurrentBranchName()` on two entirely separate `GitService` instances).

